Context: the surrounding pages work with globular sets F_0 ⇇ F_1 ⇇ F_2 ⇇ … (sources s_i, targets t_i, units σ_i, compositions μ, and constraints a, l, r). This is the setting of Grothendieck's ∞-groupoids/∞-categories as in Pursuing Stacks. The previous page tries to read a product of simplices Δ_n × Δ_{n'} as a globular cell complex, with F_0 = ∂Δ_{n'}, F_1 = Fl(Δ_n) × ∂Δ_{n'}, and so on. The next page builds the chain complex Z[F_n] with differential t_n − s_n. It then states Whitehead-type criteria (π_0 surjective/injective, π_i surjective/injective) for a map of globular ∞-groupoids f: F → F' to be a weak equivalence.

This page continues the product computation for categories C_1, C_2, C_3, C_4, regarded as 1-dimensional cell complexes (objects are 0-cells, arrows are 1-cells).

(1) Grothendieck lists candidate globular strata:
- low degree: something built from Ob C_4 and Fl_0(C_1), with Fl_0 probably meaning identity/degenerate arrows;
- next: (Fl C_1 × Ob C_2) glued along Fl_0(C_1) × Ob C_2;
- then Fl C_1 × Fl C_2 × Ob C_3;
- then F_3 = Fl C_1 × Fl C_2 × Fl C_3 × Ob C_4;
- finally F_n = Fl C_1 × … × Fl C_n.

So a k-cell is a k-tuple of arrows in the first k factors together with an object of the next factor. This is a 'lexicographic' or lax way of extracting a globular structure from the cubical cell structure of a product. The sketches support this reading:
- a 3-D subdivided grid (a product of three 1-complexes);
- a segment, a box/horn shape and a square (1- and 2-cubes);
- a cylinder whose top is collapsed to a lens/disk, i.e. a globe obtained from a cylinder.

(2) He then generalises to arbitrary pairs O_i ⊂ F_i (subcomplex inside complex). He proposes forming F_1 × … × F_n × (?) and a 'join'-like operation (O_1,F_1) * (O_2,F_2) * … * (O_n,F_n). This is the relative product of pairs, in the spirit of the modern pushout-product (Leibniz/box product) of inclusions.

(3) For Ḟ ⊂ F (boundary inside cell) he writes (F × I) ⊔_{Ḟ×I} Ḟ. This is the cylinder on F with the cylinder over the boundary collapsed back onto the boundary: the 'reduced cylinder' or globular suspension. Applied to D_n with Ḟ = ∂D_n it produces the globe D_{n+1}, with two n-hemispheres glued along ∂D_n.

Purpose: build the standard globular models (globes, products, cylinders and homotopies) needed to define the fundamental ∞-groupoid of a space or a cylinder object. With these, homotopy and weak equivalence of ∞-groupoids can be defined; the criteria on the next page then follow.
